-- title:
--   Remaining modulo-840 prime cases after the one hundred thirty-five congruence families
-- statement:
--   Let $p>2$ be prime. Assume $p\bmod840\in\{1,121,169,289,361,529\}$ and that $p$ avoids the excluded residue classes modulo each of the 135 listed moduli: 11, 19, 23, 31, 43, 47, 59, 67, 71, 79, 83, 103, 107, 127, 131, 139, 143, 151, 163, 167, 179, 187, 191, 199, 211, 223, 227, 239, 247, 251, 263, 271, 283, 299, 307, 311, 319, 323, 331, 347, 359, 367, 379, 383, 391, 403, 407, 419, 431, 439, 443, 451, 463, 467, 487, 491, 499, 503, 523, 527, 547, 551, 559, 563, 571, 583, 587, 599, 607, 611, 619, 631, 643, 647, 659, 667, 671, 683, 691, 703, 719, 727, 731, 739, 743, 751, 767, 779, 787, 799, 803, 811, 823, 827, 839, 851, 859, 863, 871, 883, 887, 899, 907, 911, 919, 923, 943, 947, 967, 971, 979, 983, 991, 1003, 1007, 1019, 1027, 1031, 1039, 1051, 1063, 1067, 1079, 1087, 1091, 1103, 1111, 1123, 1139, 1147, 1151, 1159, 1163, 1171, 1187. The remaining assertion is that there are natural numbers $1\le x<y<z$ with $4/p=1/x+1/y+1/z$ in $\mathbb Q$. This is the open residual frontier of Erdős Problem 242, obtained from `ErdosStraus242.hard_core_840_after_113_sieves` by removing the twenty-two families `family_mod1003` … `family_mod1187` — completing the $a=1$ sweep over ALL moduli $\equiv 3 \pmod 4$ coprime to $840$ below $1200$. Together with those proved families it recovers the previous frontier; the strict denominator convention is retained.
-- source:
--   Erdős Problem 242, https://www.erdosproblems.com/242, restricted from the mission frontier ErdosStraus242.hard_core_840_after_113_sieves. The twenty-two additional exclusions come from the Bloom–Elsholtz $a=1$ parametrization classes at the moduli 1003, 1007, 1019, 1027, 1031, 1039, 1051, 1063, 1067, 1079, 1087, 1091, 1103, 1111, 1123, 1139, 1147, 1151, 1159, 1163, 1171, 1187, p. 239, https://www.math.tugraz.at/~elsholtz/WWW/papers/bloom-elsholtz-naw5-2022-23-4-237.pdf. This residual formulation is a decomposition made here, not a quoted result.

